# Commit and push after changes

After completing a task that modified files, **commit and push** unless the user explicitly says not to (e.g. "don't commit yet").

## Repositories

| Work area | Directory | Remote | Branch |
|-----------|-----------|--------|--------|
| App | `quotation-invoice-app/` | `https://github.com/Meemkhaan/smart_invoicify.git` | `master` |
| Landing / marketing | workspace root and `smart-invoicify/` | `https://github.com/Meemkhaan/smart-invoicify.git` | `main` |

Run git commands from the correct directory. The landing site may exist in both the workspace root and the nested `smart-invoicify/` clone—keep them in sync when both are updated.

## Workflow

1. Finish the requested change and verify it works (typecheck/tests when relevant).
2. `git status` and `git diff` in the affected repo(s).
3. Stage only relevant files; never commit secrets (`.env`, credentials).
4. Commit with a concise message focused on **why**.
5. Push to the remote branch (`git push origin master` or `main`).
6. If push is rejected, pull/rebase then push again.

## Exceptions

- User explicitly asks not to commit or push.
- No file changes were made (question-only or review-only tasks).
- Commit would fail hooks—fix and create a **new** commit; do not amend unless user rules allow it.
